WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.200
British actor Dan Stevens dives head first
into the role of the Marvel comics character

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.780
David Haller, whose diagnosis of schizophrenia
may actually be masking his mutant abilities,

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.260
in the TV show Legion.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.200
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to be revealing

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.460
some fascinating facts (you might not know)
about the making of the mind-bending new series

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.700
from Fargo showrunner Noah Hawley.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.070
Legion the TV series didn't start out as we
know it today.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:29.700
Back in 2014, following the success of the
first season of Fargo, the producers behind

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.270
the X-Men film franchise approached Fargo
showrunner Noah Hawley about making an X-Men

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.270
TV series.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:40.690
But at that stage, the story of David Haller,
aka Legion, was not the one on the table.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:45.300
The original idea put forward to Hawley was
a show about the Hellfire Club, which previously

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.800
appeared on the big screen in X-Men: First
Class.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.239
However, Hawley, who grew up reading X-Men
comics, wasn't keen on bringing that world

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:55.899
to the small screen or in making a straightforward
comic-book adaptation.

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:01.359
Instead, he wanted to add something fresh,
playful, and unexpected to the genre so he

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:05.960
chose the character of David Haller which
allowed him to explore identity, question

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.510
reality, and make a show where the audience
would do the same.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:14.450
In Marvel's comics, David Haller has multiple
personalities and is the super-powerful mutant

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:15.890
son of Professor X.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.550
The character was created in the mid-1980s
by Chris Claremont and Bill Sienkiewicz.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.960
By the way, Fox did start developing another
TV show about the Hellfire Club with different

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.869
showrunners, but that particular idea was
shut down when Fox decided the draft Hellfire

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:32.190
script worked better as a feature film than
a TV series/didn't work as a TV series, and

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:36.230
the showrunners began work on the new series
24 Legacy instead.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.479
When it came to developing Legion, Noah Hawley
was inspired by a variety of British films

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.399
from the 60s and 70s including Quadrophenia
and Stanley Kubrick's A Clockwork Orange.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:49.799
In fact, he wanted the series to have a similar
vibe to the kind of movies British actor Terence

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:52.049
Stamp made in the 1960s.

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:56.210
However, because Hawley views his new show
as a kind of fable, and because it focuses

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.090
on protagonist David Haller's very subjective
and fractured experience of the world, Legion

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.210
isn't set in one particular time period or
location.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:09.659
So, its surreal look ranges from the past
to present-day right into the future.

00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:14.640
Another major influence on the series was
British rock band Pink Floyd and their classic

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.620
1973 album The Dark Side of the Moon, which
Hawley describes as the 'soundscape of mental

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:19.620
illness'.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.420
In fact, the character that actress Rachel
Keller plays is named after one of Pink Floyd's

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.160
founding members, Syd Barrett, who, like David
Haller, may have been schizophrenic.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.530
The band also inspired the show's music, which
is a mix of electronic, orchestral, and atonal

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.000
sounds, tape effects, and music playing backwards.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.690
Indeed, composer Jeff Russo told The Marvel
Report and Nerdophiles blog that he bought

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.770
the kind of synthesizer used on Dark Side
of the Moon in order to channel the right

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:45.770
sound.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.060
Russo, who's a fan of Pink Floyd and 80s X-Men
comics, knew his music needed to capture David

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.190
Haller's sense of uncertainty about what's
real and what isn't, while moving perfectly

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.870
between those two worlds.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.010
And he found that reading neurologist Oliver
Sacks' book Hallucinations helped him understand

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.120
how people like David Haller, who hear voices,
perceive reality during auditory hallucinations.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.459
Speaking of music, to help Dan Stevens get
into the right headspace to play Haller, showrunner

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:13.340
Noah Hawley put together a special playlist
for him.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:19.659
The 160 tracks that Hawley chose were an extremely
eclectic mix ranging from music such as Pink

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:24.739
Floyd to The Muppets, 'people screaming into
bins', and 'experimental French sound art'!

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:28.961
Legion may be its own standalone show at the
moment, but you should still look out for

00:03:28.961 --> 00:03:30.760
X-Men nods in the series.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.590
In fact, a bunch of props that featured in
the X-Men movies and which are normally kept

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:38.730
in storage, were shipped to the sound stage
in Vancouver where Legion was filming.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.519
Showrunner Noah Hawley has pretty much had
carte blanche in bringing the character of

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:46.739
Legion to the small screen, so, keep your
eyes peeled throughout season one's eight

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:50.150
episodes to see which X-Men movie props he
snuck into the show!

